  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x² + 0.5 z²)/2, drawn at half its value. It is steeper along x, so learning rates above 0.8 (2/2.5) make the x swings grow until the run diverges. Below that, large rates overshoot but settle.",
    "Each step is minus learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is random and centred on zero, with size 1.5 × √((64−B)/(63B)): the spread from sampling B of 64 points, exactly zero at B = 64.",
    "The orange arrow is the step the ball takes next, against the noisy slope. The grey arrow is minus learning rate × the true slope. Both are drawn 2× their true length. The readout shows the true step length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as diverged when the loss goes above 5 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. A diverged ball is drawn at the bowl's rim, and the loss readout keeps its true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed, run number and step. Reset replays the same sequence. A new run uses the next noise values. Runs take 5 steps per second at 1× speed.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average. With small batches the ball keeps wandering near the lowest point instead of stopping on it."
  ]
}